            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the eigenvectors of a square matrix - the directions it stretches or shrinks without turning - and the eigenvalues, how much it stretches along each.\n\nA transformation applied over and over (a feedback loop, a step of a simulation, a Markov chain of states) ends up dominated by its largest eigenvalue: above 1 it grows, below 1 it dies away, and the matching eigenvector is the shape it settles into. Eigenvalues show whether a system is stable, and how fast it changes.\n\nSome matrices turn every direction - a rotation is the plain example - and then the eigenvalues come out as complex numbers, whose angle is the turn. For a symmetric matrix (a covariance, a correlation, a distance table) use np.linalg.eigh instead: it is faster, always real, and sorted.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.eig\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nA square matrix. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\neigenvalues:\nHow much the matrix stretches along each eigenvector, in no particular order. Real numbers when they all are; complex numbers when the matrix turns some direction.\n\neigenvectors:\nOne eigenvector per COLUMN, in the same order, each of length 1. Sent first. Each one's sign is arbitrary.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 2 1 / 0 3 stretches by 2 along x - the first eigenvector, the first column, is 1 0 - and by 3 along the diagonal 0.71 0.71.\n\nBottom: 0 -1 / 1 0 is a quarter turn. No direction survives it unturned, so the eigenvalues are complex: 1j and -1j, a turn of 90 degrees one way and the other. A metro re-sends both five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.eigh for symmetric matrices. np.linalg.svd works for any matrix, square or not. t.linalg.eig is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the eigenvectors of a square matrix - the directions it stretches or shrinks without turning - and the eigenvalues, how much it stretches along each.\n\nA transformation applied over and over (a feedback loop, a step of a simulation, a Markov chain of states) ends up dominated by its largest eigenvalue: above 1 it grows, below 1 it dies away, and the matching eigenvector is the shape it settles into. Eigenvalues show whether a system is stable, and how fast it changes.\n\nSome matrices turn every direction - a rotation is the plain example - and then the eigenvalues come out as complex numbers, whose angle is the turn. For a symmetric matrix (a covariance, a correlation, a distance table) use np.linalg.eigh instead: it is faster, always real, and sorted.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.eig\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nA square matrix. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\neigenvalues:\nHow much the matrix stretches along each eigenvector, in no particular order. Real numbers when they all are; complex numbers when the matrix turns some direction.\n\neigenvectors:\nOne eigenvector per COLUMN, in the same order, each of length 1. Sent first. Each one's sign is arbitrary.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 2 1 / 0 3 stretches by 2 along x - the first eigenvector, the first column, is 1 0 - and by 3 along the diagonal 0.71 0.71.\n\nBottom: 0 -1 / 1 0 is a quarter turn. No direction survives it unturned, so the eigenvalues are complex: 1j and -1j, a turn of 90 degrees one way and the other. A metro re-sends both five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.eigh for symmetric matrices. np.linalg.svd works for any matrix, square or not. t.linalg.eig is the PyTorch version."
        },
